<section id="top">
        <div class="page-shell about-page-intro">
            <span class="eyebrow">Quickler in India</span>
            <h1>No-app site inspections for Indian field teams.</h1>
            <p class="page-lede">Picture a safety supervisor walking a construction site or a depot at the start of a shift, checking scaffolding, a DG set, fire points and the vehicles in the yard. Today that often means a paper checklist on a clipboard, or a tablet app that someone has to buy, install, log into and keep charged. Quickler replaces both. The supervisor answers each check over WhatsApp, by text, voice note or photo, on the phone already in their pocket. Nothing to install, nothing to carry, no per-user fee.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight">
        <div class="content-duo">
            <div class="section-frame">
                <div class="section-header">
                    <div>
                        <p class="section-label">The yard at 7am</p>
                        <h2>A whole shift's checks, run from one WhatsApp thread.</h2>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="detail-body">
                    <p>A field team rarely inspects just one thing. A single morning round can cover the site, the plant and the fleet. Quickler runs all of it as a guided conversation, one question at a time, so the worker never has to find the right screen or scroll a long form.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Site safety walk: edge protection, housekeeping, signage, fire points</li>
                        <li>Equipment checks: DG set, crane, forklift, scaffolding, electrical boards</li>
                        <li>Vehicle walkround: tyres, lights, brakes, load security before the run</li>
                        <li>Each answer can be a tick, a short voice note, or a photo of the actual fault</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Because it is WhatsApp, a worker who is more comfortable speaking than typing can simply send a voice note, and a damaged guardrail or a leaking fitting is captured as a photo at the moment it is seen, not written up from memory hours later.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="section-frame">
                <div class="section-header">
                    <div>
                        <p class="section-label">What the office sees</p>
                        <h2>Not a stack of paper. A live record with fault status.</h2>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="detail-body">
                    <p>Paper checklists pile up in a drawer and tablet apps trap the data inside one device. Quickler turns every check into a clean, structured digital record on a manager dashboard the moment it is submitted.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Every item is marked red, amber or green, so the failures stand out instantly</li>
                        <li>The record is live and machine-readable, not a scan of a signed sheet</li>
                        <li>Photos and voice notes sit against the exact item they belong to</li>
                        <li>Export a PDF in one step when you need a document to file or send on</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>A manager in Mumbai or Bengaluru can see that the Pune site cleared its morning round and that one forklift was flagged amber, without waiting for anyone to come back to the office and hand in a form.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

